# The GST domain, as terms.
#
# Every one of these is a *runtime* term: the namespace is allocated by
# `POST /namespaces` and each predicate registered by `POST /predicates`, so
# nothing here required a line of Rust. That is the claim
# `plans/105-domain-neutrality.md` exists to make checkable — read this beside
# `packs/hospitality/ontology.ttl`, which shares none of it and loads through
# exactly the same path.
#
# Authored from the published statute and return formats, which are the
# specification (`plans/00i-licensing.md` rule 2).

@prefix gst: <https://graph-owl.dev/packs/gst#> .
@prefix rdf: <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .

gst:Supplier        rdf:type gst:Class ; gst:label "Supplier" .
gst:PurchaseInvoice rdf:type gst:Class ; gst:label "Purchase invoice (taxpayer's register)" .
gst:Gstr2bInvoice   rdf:type gst:Class ; gst:label "GSTR-2B invoice (as filed by the supplier)" .
gst:Section         rdf:type gst:Class ; gst:label "Statutory section" .

# **The canonical business entity — Plan 109 Slice 2.** One per real invoice,
# not one per source that reported it. `PurchaseInvoice`/`Gstr1Invoice`/
# `Gstr2bInvoice` (below) remain exactly what they were — the per-source
# evidence layer, unrenamed — and this is the subject a reviewer or a query
# reaches first: "what do we know about this invoice across every source",
# one traversal instead of three separately-typed subjects joined by a
# shared key value. See `plans/109-gst-canonical-invoice-model.md` decision 4
# for the full wiring diagram.
gst:Invoice         rdf:type gst:Class ; gst:label "Invoice (canonical, across every source)" .

# **A GSTR-1/IFF filing — one per (supplier, return period), not one per
# invoice line.** Today's per-line `Gstr1Invoice` records each repeat
# `period`/`filedDate` identically for every invoice from the same filing;
# this is the subject those two properties move to, so "was this filing
# late" is asked once rather than answered identically many times over.
gst:Gstr1Filing     rdf:type gst:Class ; gst:label "GSTR-1/IFF filing (one per supplier, per period)" .

# **A GSTR-2B statement — one per return period, not one per supplier.**
# Unlike GSTR-1 (the supplier's own filing), GSTR-2B is auto-drafted by the
# authority *for the recipient*, aggregating every supplier's filings for
# that period. `generatedDate` is optional: no fixture or API payload field
# has been confirmed to carry it.
gst:Gstr2bStatement rdf:type gst:Class ; gst:label "GSTR-2B statement (one per return period)" .

# **The taxpayer this deployment represents — a single-tenant pack context
# anchor, not a globally reusable identity.** Exactly one instance exists,
# `gst:recipient-self`, and every `Gstr2bStatement` points at it. Must not
# be used as the basis for cross-tenant identity resolution; if multi-tenant
# GST support is introduced later, the recipient subject becomes
# tenant-scoped and can carry the taxpayer's own GSTIN.
gst:Recipient       rdf:type gst:Class ; gst:label "Recipient (this deployment's own taxpayer identity)" .

# **The third evidence source, and the one that turns "unmatched" into a
# reason** — `plans/108-books-gstr1-gstr2b-reconciliation.md` Slice 1.
#
# GSTR-1 is what the supplier *declared*; GSTR-2B is what the authority
# *made available* to the recipient for a period. Without this class the two
# very different causes of an invoice's absence from 2B — the supplier never
# filed it, versus the supplier filed it and it never reached this taxpayer —
# collapse into one finding, and they need opposite next actions from a CA.
#
# **There is deliberately no `gst:Gstr2aInvoice`.** 2A is a revolving view
# over the same supplier-declared data this class already carries; modelling
# both would give the graph two sources for one fact with no query that needs
# the distinction.
gst:Gstr1Invoice    rdf:type gst:Class ; gst:label "GSTR-1/IFF invoice (as declared by the supplier)" .
gst:Provision       rdf:type gst:Class ; gst:label "Dated provision (a rule in force between two dates)" .
gst:Policy          rdf:type gst:Class ; gst:label "Matching policy (this pack's own judgement, not statute)" .

# Events, because two of the six conditions are about *when* rather than
# *what*. An invoice is a record; a purchase and a payment are things that
# happened, each with its own time anchor.
gst:PurchaseEvent   rdf:type gst:Class ; gst:label "Purchase event" .
gst:PaymentEvent    rdf:type gst:Class ; gst:label "Payment event" .

# The third event, and it needs **no new predicates at all** — it reuses
# `onInvoice` and `atTime` exactly as the two above do (Plan 108 Slice 8).
#
# Section 16(2)(b) makes receipt of the goods or services a condition in its
# own right. That is a different *kind* of question from every other rule in
# this pack: the others ask whether a document contains an invoice, this one
# asks whether something has happened yet. An invoice can pass every document
# check and still not be claimable, because the goods had not arrived.
gst:GoodsReceipt    rdf:type gst:Class ; gst:label "Goods or services receipt event" .

gst:supplierGstin rdf:type gst:Property ; gst:label "supplier GSTIN" .
gst:invoiceNumber rdf:type gst:Property ; gst:label "invoice number" .

# **What two records are the same invoice by**, as distinct from what the
# invoice is *called*. A supplier writes `INV-2023/01` and the taxpayer's clerk
# keys `INV202301`; an exact join on the number sees two unrelated invoices and
# reports the register row as never filed *and* the 2B row as never booked —
# two false findings, opposite in direction, about one invoice that matched.
# Case and punctuation are removed; leading zeros deliberately are not, because
# `INV-001` and `INV-1` are different invoices in plenty of numbering schemes
# and a wrong match claims credit against the wrong one.
gst:invoiceKey    rdf:type gst:Property ; gst:label "normalized invoice matching key" .
gst:invoiceDate   rdf:type gst:Property ; gst:label "invoice date" .
gst:taxableValue  rdf:type gst:Property ; gst:label "taxable value" .
gst:taxAmount     rdf:type gst:Property ; gst:label "tax amount" .
gst:period        rdf:type gst:Property ; gst:label "return period" .
gst:itcAvailable  rdf:type gst:Property ; gst:label "input tax credit available (as reported in GSTR-2B)" .

# **The one new scalar Plan 108 needs**, and the reason a late filing is an
# explainable fact rather than an inferred one. An invoice dated 7 July with
# `filedDate = "2026-08-18"` is *why* it cannot be in July's 2B — the graph
# never has to know what a cut-off is, because the 2B document for a period
# either contains the invoice or it does not, and that document is the
# authority's own answer to whether the filing landed in time.
gst:filedDate     rdf:type gst:Property ; gst:label "date the supplier filed the return declaring this invoice" .
gst:reverseCharge rdf:type gst:Property ; gst:label "reverse-charge flag (as reported in GSTR-2B)" .
gst:governedBy    rdf:type gst:Property ; gst:label "governed by" .

# The provision vocabulary — what makes the law queryable rather than encoded.
gst:effectiveFrom rdf:type gst:Property ; gst:label "effective from" .
gst:capPercent    rdf:type gst:Property ; gst:label "provisional credit cap (percent)" .
gst:citation      rdf:type gst:Property ; gst:label "citation" .

# Event vocabulary.
gst:onInvoice     rdf:type gst:Property ; gst:label "on invoice" .
gst:atTime        rdf:type gst:Property ; gst:label "at time" .
gst:amount        rdf:type gst:Property ; gst:label "amount" .

# `gst:Supplier` was already declared above and never instantiated — every
# invoice carried its GSTIN as a bare literal, unreachable by a graph walk.
# `issuedBy` is the edge that closes that: `plans/105c-gst-causal-graph.md`.
gst:issuedBy      rdf:type gst:Property ; gst:label "issued by" .

# The canonical-entity edges — Plan 109 Slice 2. All three point *down*, from
# the canonical `gst:Invoice` to the per-source evidence record it was built
# from — never the reverse, and never collapsing the per-source record out of
# the graph. `reflectedIn` is deliberately reused at two levels: canonical
# Invoice to its `Gstr2bInvoice` line, and that line on to the
# `Gstr2bStatement` it came from — both mean the same thing, "the authority's
# own record of this," at different granularity.
gst:recordedIn    rdf:type gst:Property ; gst:label "recorded in (purchase register)" .
gst:appearsIn     rdf:type gst:Property ; gst:label "appears in (GSTR-1/IFF)" .
gst:reflectedIn   rdf:type gst:Property ; gst:label "reflected in (GSTR-2B)" .

# The one genuinely new hop: a declared GSTR-1 line to the filing it came
# from, and that filing to the supplier who filed it.
gst:filedIn       rdf:type gst:Property ; gst:label "filed in (which GSTR-1/IFF filing)" .
gst:filedBy       rdf:type gst:Property ; gst:label "filed by" .

# A GSTR-2B statement to the recipient it was generated for — always
# `gst:recipient-self` today, since this pack is single-tenant.
gst:generatedFor  rdf:type gst:Property ; gst:label "generated for" .

# When the authority generated the statement — optional, since no current
# source has been confirmed to carry it.
gst:generatedDate rdf:type gst:Property ; gst:label "date the authority generated this statement" .
